Automate Log Cleanup with Bash – A Real DevOps Use Case

Today, let’s dive into a real-world automation example: log cleanup using a Bash script. This is a small but powerful task that every DevOps engineer eventually needs.

🧹 Why automate log cleanup?
Prevent disk space issues
Keep your server clean and organized
Avoid log overload in production environments
Reduce manual maintenance


🧾 Here’s a Bash script I use:
#!/bin/bash
find /var/log -type f -name "*.log" -mtime +7 -exec rm -f {} \;
echo "Old logs deleted!"
✅ This script finds .log files older than 7 days in /var/log and deletes them.

⏰ Want to automate this daily?
 Use cron to schedule it:
crontab -e
Then add:
0 1 * * * /path/to/script.sh
🔄 This will run the cleanup every day at 1:00 AM.

💡 Takeaway: DevOps is all about saving time and reducing risk. Even small scripts like this can add huge value!
